00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:46.580
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.205
Thank you so much
for joining us today.

00:04:48.205 --> 00:04:49.704
Pretty excited to
be talking to you.

00:04:49.704 --> 00:04:51.540
I think the stuff you
do and the company

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.480
you work for do some
really interesting stuff.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.680
But tell us a little about
you, and who you are,

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.480
and what you do, and
all that kind of stuff.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:00.530
TIM KELTON: Sure.

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:02.980
I'm an engineer and part
of the founding team

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.360
here at Descartes.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:08.520
And my main focus is Cloud
architecture, and then building

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:13.030
and scaling what we call
our Cloud supercomputer,

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:16.570
which is basically a
forecasting platform.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:23.330
And so I end up using a range
of Google Cloud Platform tools

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.340
to accomplish this goal.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.730
And that's mainly to
see how the Earth is

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:29.670
changing every single day.

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:34.480
And before Descartes, I was
a research and development

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:39.380
engineer for 15 years at Los
Alamos National Laboratory.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:41.695
And there, I worked on a
wide range of projects,

00:05:41.695 --> 00:05:46.510
but it was focused around deep--
the most recent project that

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:49.530
lasted about six years was
focused around deep learning

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:53.770
and applying deep learning
to real world problem areas,

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:55.250
like satellite imagery.

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:57.520
And then a portion
of that team left

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:58.859
Los Alamos to start Descartes.

00:05:58.859 --> 00:05:59.775
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:05:59.775 --> 00:06:03.060
So you mentioned
that Descartes Lab,

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.770
it allows you to see
how the Earth changes.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:09.050
What does that mean?

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.030
TIM KELTON: Yeah,
so we're building

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:13.260
a living atlas of the world.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:17.220
And how we're doing that is
we're applying machine learning

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:22.050
with massive scale data sets,
such as satellite imagery,

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:26.870
also data sets like global
weather, pricing, [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:29.010
truth, even customer data.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:32.140
And then we feed that into
our forecasting platform.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:37.990
And then we can build our
machine learning models

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.880
and constantly try and
improve those models.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.500
And this allows
us to not just see

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:46.630
what's happened
historically, but also it

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:49.840
lets us forecast
into the future.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.400
Kind of interestingly
enough, we mainly

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.970
are focused on areas that humans
just could never scale to do,

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.210
things like global-scale
food production.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:00.810
You just couldn't
get enough people

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:04.930
on the ground counting
how productive a field is

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:06.050
all over the world.

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:07.220
It just isn't scalable.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:10.300
So we're using satellite
data in that way.

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:11.840
And it's really exciting.

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:14.950
We're only 20 months old,
so it's a really early stage

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:16.360
company.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.360
But we're moving really quickly.

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:22.150
MARK MANDEL: So exactly how
much data are you talking about?

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:24.650
You're talking about satellite
imagery and forecast weather.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:27.810
I mean, how big are
these data sets?

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.140
TIM KELTON: So the
data sets vary.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:33.190
Right now we currently
have compressed

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:36.600
around three and a
half petabytes of data

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.560
that we're storing in a
Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:40.990
MARK MANDEL: Oh, only a couple
of petabytes, it's fine.

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:43.800
TIM KELTON: Yeah.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.550
MARK MANDEL: And that
process is, like,

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:48.180
that's over a number of years?

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:49.282
Is that, like, recently?

00:07:49.282 --> 00:07:51.240
Like, what's that sort
of time series, as well?

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:55.410
TIM KELTON: Yeah, that's
an interesting area.

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:59.530
As most people that do machine
learning or use tools like Cafe

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:03.240
or TensorFlow can tell
you, a really big challenge

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:07.400
with machine learning tools is
having an accurate set of data

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:11.290
to train the models and then
improve the models' accuracy.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:14.180
And when you're doing things
like looking at the Earth,

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:20.520
having a really large time
window of data helps out a lot.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:24.730
And often with satellite
data, training sets

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:26.290
can be extremely varied.

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:29.690
And things like weather cycles
are not just repeatable year

00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:30.290
after year.

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:32.970
They vary quite a
bit through time.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:37.450
So the longest continuous
observations of the Earth

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:40.000
came from NASA's
Landsat constellation.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.210
And that was launched in 1973.

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:46.900
And that's actually available,
thanks to the Google Earth

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:49.570
Engine team, on Google Cloud.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:53.180
And so our first big project
really early on in the company

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:56.800
was we basically saw we
needed a lot of backtest data

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:00.640
to be able to test
models throughout time.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:03.810
And we basically
processed 43 years

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:07.160
of this NASA Landsat imagery.

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:11.420
So in pixel terms, that's going
to be 2.8 petapixels of data.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.810
In data terms, that's
about a petabyte of data.

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:18.560
And so we did all of that
on Google Compute Engine.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.670
And that was about just
a little under 16 hours.

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:26.340
And that's using 30,000
virtual CPU cores.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:30.150
And that was all
on preemptible VMs.

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:32.560
So it was quite sizable.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:36.950
And I should say that as
impressive as Google Compute

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:39.810
Engine scaling up, probably
the most interesting part to us

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:46.090
was GCE's network and how well
the network actually scaled.

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:49.630
We were actually seeing,
for most of that 16 hours,

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:53.380
over 20 gigabytes a second going
into Google Compute Engine,

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:55.920
and then over 8 gigs
in parallel going out

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:58.200
of Google Compute Engine.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:02.340
And we've worked in
places-- most of us

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:05.270
come from Los Alamos,
or have worked

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:08.500
on other large-scale
supercomputing clusters.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:11.550
And those types of
numbers are on par

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:14.720
with anything you would see in
a high-performance supercomputer

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:15.690
system today.

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:19.640
So we basically are
using Google to build

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:24.430
the same types of systems that
we were used to working with.

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:27.550
Google recently actually had
a paper about their networking

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:29.920
called-- I think it's maglev.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:31.710
And so that was
really fascinating.

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:33.887
As an end user, we
saw how it scaled,

00:10:33.887 --> 00:10:35.220
and that was really interesting.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:37.604
But then reading
the paper of how

00:10:37.604 --> 00:10:39.270
all those components
were built together

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:41.060
was pretty fascinating.

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:43.420
MARK MANDEL: So you were
processing that entire petabyte

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:44.760
data set during that time?

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:45.890
TIM KELTON: Yeah, we
were running that all

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:47.562
through our processing pipeline.

00:10:47.562 --> 00:10:50.452
MARK MANDEL: And how much,
roundabouts, is that cost?

00:10:50.452 --> 00:10:52.660
I mean, you talked a little
bit about preemptible VMs

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:53.450
there, but--

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:56.160
TIM KELTON: So preemptibles
dramatically reduce your costs

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:01.000
if your workload doesn't
need to last over 24 hours.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:02.890
So for this use
case, our whole goal

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:04.890
was to get it done
in under a day,

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:08.340
and they were great for that.

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:13.130
When we ran this in early
2015, the cost for just Compute

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:16.380
was just under $10,000.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:19.730
But then I recently just looked
at-- there was recent pricing

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:22.070
drops in Compute Engine.

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:26.570
And so those prices now
would be about $3,600.

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:29.910
So that kind of shows the
difference in pricing.

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:31.380
That's not the data storage.

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:34.440
The data storage is a
little more than that.

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:37.185
MARK MANDEL: But to go
through a petabyte, that's

00:11:37.185 --> 00:11:37.810
not too shabby.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:40.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, that
is pretty amazing, actually.

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:45.450
So you mentioned real quick
about preventable VMs.

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:47.020
MARK MANDEL: Preemptible?

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:47.680
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What did I say?

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:48.510
MARK MANDEL: You
said preventable.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:49.885
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Preventable, no.

00:11:49.885 --> 00:11:51.300
Preemptible VMs, sorry.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:52.750
Why did you choose them?

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:54.380
What is the big thing?

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:59.010
I understand that it is more
affordable than the normal VMs.

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.510
But what kind of
restrictions and what kind

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:04.610
of design decisions did
you take into account?

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:06.950
TIM KELTON: Yeah, so
preemptibles, at maximum,

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:09.310
will only last you for 24 hours.

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:14.280
And so you need to be processing
some sort of processing task

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:16.217
that's going to obviously
last less than that.

00:12:16.217 --> 00:12:18.300
So you're not going to run
a production web server

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.110
or database server on that.

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:23.180
But for really large
processing workloads,

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:26.670
such as machine
learning, model training,

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:30.390
having a lot of
pre-processing pipelines,

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:31.935
they're a great
use case for that.

00:12:31.935 --> 00:12:35.650
For us, we use
asynchronous task queues

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:39.620
to load data, or the
processing we're going to do,

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:42.370
into the task queue,
and then connect up

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:45.940
all these preemptible
instances via instance groups.

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:48.680
And then you can scale up
and down the instance groups.

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:53.280
And now you can scale up and
down the instance groups inside

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:55.550
of a zone in different regions.

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:56.670
So that's quite useful.

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:57.420
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.310
So you were able
to sort of leverage

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:02.340
the power of preemptible VMs
to basically reduce your costs,

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:03.760
more than anything else

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:05.670
TIM KELTON: Yeah,
it's a huge cost

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:09.510
reduction compared
to just running

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:12.570
a regular VM for a few hours.

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:15.950
So we use them quite heavily in
all of our processing pipelines

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:18.550
now as a cost reduction.

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:21.346
But also with machine
learning, a lot of times

00:13:21.346 --> 00:13:23.720
you're training and retraining
a model to try and improve

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:24.840
your accuracy.

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:29.770
So we have a lot of really
large-scale bulk workloads.

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:33.550
And we've kind of taken the
road of not going down the path

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:35.670
of using something
like Hadoop or things,

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:39.750
because we have a lot of
C and C++ and Python code.

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:46.800
And so instead, we basically
use Google Cloud Storage

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:50.100
as our large
distributed file system.

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:54.760
And we know that will scale
way beyond our current needs.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:57.380
And it has really, really
fast network access

00:13:57.380 --> 00:14:01.600
from Compute Engine, so we
can connect real quickly.

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:07.680
So it has a lot of benefits
over trying to get C or C++

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.014
or Python code to work inside
of something like Hadoop.

00:14:11.014 --> 00:14:13.180
MARK MANDEL: But the issue,
obviously-- or maybe not

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:15.270
obviously-- but the issue
with preemptible VMs

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:16.320
is they could shut down.

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.880
How do you deal
with that happening?

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:20.935
Is there, like, a retry
mechanism you use?

00:14:20.935 --> 00:14:22.350
Or how do you deal with that?

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:24.590
TIM KELTON: Yeah, we
use some pretty basic

00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:26.140
open source task queues.

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:29.160
We use a Python
queue called Celery.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:32.230
And that has a backend
persistent store.

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:34.310
You can configure a
bunch of different ones.

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:34.950
We use Redis.

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:37.440
We're pretty big fans of Redis.

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:39.560
And so we write all the
tasks into the queue.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:42.210
And then basically,
if they still

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:45.470
are not done within a certain
time, you can retry the tasks

00:14:45.470 --> 00:14:49.780
or rerun certain tasks over
again and make sure you've

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:51.070
processed all of your work.

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:53.950
Our workloads are,
in many situations,

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:56.540
they kind of fall into this
embarrassingly parallel

00:14:56.540 --> 00:14:57.450
problem.

00:14:57.450 --> 00:15:00.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
you mentioned that you

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:03.300
were using asynchronous tasks.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:05.050
Can you talk a little
bit more about that?

00:15:05.050 --> 00:15:06.960
Are you using Pub/Sub?

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:08.270
Are you using task queues?

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:11.662
What kind of product
are you using?

00:15:11.662 --> 00:15:13.620
TIM KELTON: We just use
an open source library.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:14.950
It's called Celery.

00:15:14.950 --> 00:15:16.610
It's a Python library.

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:18.550
And that has
configurable backends.

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:22.180
And on that backend we
use Redis in-memory.

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:24.470
And that's all just running
on a regular-- that's

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:28.350
on a non-preemptible
instance, because we do not

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:29.670
want that to go away.

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:35.260
And then for monitoring, we use
the Redis Stackdriver agent.

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:37.830
And that keeps track of how
many concurrent connections

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:43.250
and how much load for various
threads on the Redis cluster

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:44.987
that's running there.

00:15:44.987 --> 00:15:46.820
That's kind of a great
way to see if there's

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:48.380
a sudden spike or change.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:50.070
When that's kind
of your central hub

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:52.740
that all your work
runs around, you

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:55.480
need that to really
be up and be reliable.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:56.240
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:57.740
So we talked a bit
about, obviously,

00:15:57.740 --> 00:15:59.150
that initial data set.

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:01.837
I'm guessing you have ongoing
data that's coming in now.

00:16:01.837 --> 00:16:04.420
As your company's expanded, you
have more and more information

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:05.670
coming in on an ongoing basis.

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:06.700
Would that be right?

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:07.890
TIM KELTON: Correct.

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:11.610
We're processing data probably
right now as we're talking.

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:13.900
We're basically
constantly ingesting

00:16:13.900 --> 00:16:16.630
new data in our
processing pipelines,

00:16:16.630 --> 00:16:18.850
literally from hundreds
of different satellites,

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:23.300
not just those eight
NASA Landsat satellites.

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:26.200
Right now, we're at about
three and a half petabytes

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:29.090
of compressed data.

00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:33.540
And a lot of that's
public satellite imagery

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:37.800
that's open-- NASA, European
Space Agency, various people

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:38.720
like that.

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:42.970
But then we also have
private partners, as well.

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:46.240
And so we're constantly,
as we get new updates

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:48.680
to various geographic
regions, or we're

00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:52.200
working on a new problem case
maybe inside of agriculture,

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:55.740
or studying a new crop that's in
a different part of the world,

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:57.490
we'll look at different
sets of imagery

00:16:57.490 --> 00:17:01.480
that we might not always
hit every single day.

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:03.970
We have about five
terabytes a day

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:06.300
that just constantly flows in.

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:08.619
And that varies from day to day.

00:17:08.619 --> 00:17:11.750
But in the future, we're
expecting this to basically

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:15.310
be over 30 petabytes in
the next year and a half.

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:19.260
And that's kind of a cool thing
in space right now, or in space

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:21.010
and technology.

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:25.300
Most of your listeners probably
watch YouTube videos of SpaceX,

00:17:25.300 --> 00:17:29.050
and SpaceX landing rockets on
a floating barge in the middle

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:30.170
of the Pacific Ocean.

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:31.530
It's pretty awesome.

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:34.290
But from a cost
perspective, a big part

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:37.550
of your cost to getting
satellites up into orbit

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:41.442
is actually the rocket launches
and making them reusable.

00:17:41.442 --> 00:17:42.900
And then at the
other side of that,

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:46.150
you have smaller and
smaller satellites.

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:48.640
And so they're made with more
and more commodity parts.

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:51.760
They're often called CubeSats.

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:54.080
An example of that would
be a Silicon Valley

00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:55.670
startup called Planet.

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:59.250
Or Google has a team
called Terra Bella.

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:01.750
And they have what
they call a smallsat.

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:03.189
So you have that
on the other end.

00:18:03.189 --> 00:18:04.980
So we're going to get
more and more sensors

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:07.590
in the next few years
going up into space, seeing

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.430
the Earth every single day.

00:18:09.430 --> 00:18:11.740
And teams like ours
will be able to look

00:18:11.740 --> 00:18:15.280
at how the Earth is
changing every single day,

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:18.650
instead of people manually
downloading an image

00:18:18.650 --> 00:18:21.390
and trying to
compare one to one.

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:25.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So going
back to your stack, what

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:29.820
is the thing that is running
on your preemptible machines?

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:32.410
Are you running
Python directly there?

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:33.550
Are you using containers?

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:35.980
What kind of jobs do you run?

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:38.920
TIM KELTON: We use
both raw code, where

00:18:38.920 --> 00:18:42.710
we use startup scripts
in the GCE instances,

00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:45.360
and we use containers
on some of the more

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:47.470
involved configurations
that we want

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:50.950
to maintain consistency
and make sure a running,

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:52.980
tested version of
the code is operating

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:55.270
in a way we're expecting.

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:58.110
We'll use startup scripts
with instance groups,

00:18:58.110 --> 00:19:01.690
so they could either connect
to Google Container Registry

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:04.080
and pull the container
down, because that's really,

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:06.570
really quick access to
pull that container,

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:09.510
because it's just sitting in
Google Cloud Storage inside

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:10.770
of our project.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:14.290
Or if we run a startup script,
then you're kind of just

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:16.830
defining scripts
to run and install.

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:19.340
But most of our
workload is Python

00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:23.810
or C or C wrapped
in Python, Cython,

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:25.800
for most of our workloads.

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:30.630
And I think Python's a pretty
popular language in the machine

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:31.990
learning world these days.

00:19:31.990 --> 00:19:32.740
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:19:32.740 --> 00:19:34.230
So I'm actually really curious.

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:37.430
You've probably looked at more
satellite data than, I don't

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:39.314
know, maybe a lot of people.

00:19:39.314 --> 00:19:41.980
I'm kind of curious to know what
are the most interesting things

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:44.310
you've managed to find
out about the planet

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:45.790
or society as a whole.

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:48.060
Like, what have you seen in
those imagery that's kind

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:49.184
of been really interesting?

00:19:51.567 --> 00:19:53.150
TIM KELTON: Yeah,
that's a tough-- you

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:55.620
see all types of random things.

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:57.510
The funniest-- maybe not funny.

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:59.590
It's very obvious after a while.

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:03.180
You don't realize how cloudy the
Earth actually is in many times

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:04.070
of the day.

00:20:04.070 --> 00:20:06.330
Often when you're
trying to find an image,

00:20:06.330 --> 00:20:09.720
if there's a big sporting
event or something like that,

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:11.530
you have a cloud
actually blocking.

00:20:11.530 --> 00:20:15.090
So we actually have a technique
for being able to process

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:17.080
and fuse together
many different scenes

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:21.350
to remove the clouds over time.

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:24.410
But yeah, I'm trying to
think of something really,

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:26.190
really bizarre I've seen.

00:20:26.190 --> 00:20:30.640
One of our co-founders,
he actually

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:34.850
located the spot and the
time when the original

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:38.260
"Star-- the original
"Star Wars" movie,

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:40.670
where it was actually filmed.

00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:42.200
He went all the
way back and found

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:44.420
this, because we have
this massive archive,

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:46.480
basically since 1973.

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:50.087
And so he found all the exact
images from that location.

00:20:50.087 --> 00:20:51.420
MARK MANDEL: That's pretty cool.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:57.370
I guess more day to day, I know
you do a lot around food yields

00:20:57.370 --> 00:20:58.270
as well, I think.

00:20:58.270 --> 00:20:59.470
Is that right?

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:00.370
TIM KELTON: Correct.

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:02.600
Our first main
products are actually

00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:05.060
focused a little bit
more in agriculture,

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:09.170
and basically modeling
the global food supply.

00:21:09.170 --> 00:21:13.360
We look down on the earth, and
we can see various fields all

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:17.140
across a growing region,
not just a small county,

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:20.390
but we could see a whole
state or whole country,

00:21:20.390 --> 00:21:21.850
or even the whole globe.

00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:24.440
And we can see that
every single day.

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:28.610
So organizations like the
US Department of Agriculture

00:21:28.610 --> 00:21:31.870
will manually send out
surveys to farmers.

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:34.890
And they'll get a small
statistical sampling

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:38.940
of the population and how the
food production is growing.

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:41.570
But we basically are
seeing every field, all

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:43.490
across the globe,
every single day,

00:21:43.490 --> 00:21:48.450
and taking a measurement of the
actual health of the fields.

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:53.720
And so that allows us to
not just classify the fields

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:55.930
and what they are, if it's
a corn field or a soybean

00:21:55.930 --> 00:21:58.000
field or a wheat
field, but also to be

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.920
able to look at how much is
that field going to produce

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:02.100
and what's our estimate.

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:05.300
And that leads us into what we
have as more of a forecasting

00:22:05.300 --> 00:22:06.256
product.

00:22:06.256 --> 00:22:08.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That is
actually very interesting.

00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:10.830
Do you use any
kind of forecasting

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:14.250
to be able to predict
things like, well, given

00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:18.240
the kind of fields we have and
given the weather forecasts

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:21.510
that we have, there's
going to be a lack of food

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:24.440
in some country in a couple
months, something like that?

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:26.730
Is that something
that you've ever used?

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:28.980
TIM KELTON: I don't think
we're going quite that far

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:31.580
down the analysis,
like is there going

00:22:31.580 --> 00:22:35.420
to be famine outbreak
in country X or Y.

00:22:35.420 --> 00:22:38.850
It's definitely something
that could be possible.

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:41.830
Right now we're looking
at overall yields.

00:22:41.830 --> 00:22:45.900
There's obviously a lot of--
how much production of corn

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:50.810
might there be in the United
States in this growing season,

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:53.770
or in South America during
this growing season?

00:22:53.770 --> 00:22:56.600
But longer term,
we're hoping to expand

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:59.500
to more and more crops and
more and more geographic

00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:02.400
regions, basically.

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:07.770
And we also actually have a
new mobile app that we built.

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:09.200
We actually built
it on Firebase.

00:23:09.200 --> 00:23:11.640
We have an iOS and Android app.

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:15.030
And anyone can
actually pull that down

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:17.090
and see our delayed forecast.

00:23:17.090 --> 00:23:20.840
It's not in real time,
it's a week behind.

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:23.950
And they can actually use
that to look up that location

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:28.360
and see what we're predicting
to be the yield of corn,

00:23:28.360 --> 00:23:33.050
say, for that given county in
Iowa or someplace like that.

00:23:33.050 --> 00:23:34.030
And you can even vote.

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:35.530
And you can say,
well, we actually

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:39.240
think you're totally wrong, or
yes, that seems about right.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:41.190
And you can kind of
provide feedback there.

00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:41.940
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:43.520
All right, that
was really great.

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:45.549
Before we wrap up here,
is there anything else

00:23:45.549 --> 00:23:47.590
you want to mention, maybe
something we've missed

00:23:47.590 --> 00:23:49.631
or something you want to
kind of mention or plug?

00:23:52.052 --> 00:23:53.880
TIM KELTON: That's
the main things.

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:56.680
Moving forward, we're
really excited about some

00:23:56.680 --> 00:23:59.760
of the new changes on
Kubernetes and being

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:02.490
able to actually
run jobs, and then

00:24:02.490 --> 00:24:04.760
having auto scaling on jobs.

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:08.120
So we are hoping that more and
more of our daily processing

00:24:08.120 --> 00:24:11.520
pipeline will be able
to work that way.

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:13.430
So that's pretty exciting.

00:24:13.430 --> 00:24:17.390
And then, we should be
having more and more updates,

00:24:17.390 --> 00:24:19.570
obviously, with our mobile
app and our website.

00:24:19.570 --> 00:24:23.880
So if you're excited about
corn yields or soybean yields,

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:26.560
you'll be able to
key in on that.

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:28.970
I'm not sure what we're
predicting in the Bay Area.

00:24:28.970 --> 00:24:32.330
I'm guessing it's pretty
minimal right now.

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:34.510
But yeah, we'll constantly
have new updates.

00:24:34.510 --> 00:24:36.700
And we'll always be
improving our models

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:41.470
and working to try and make
them more and more accurate

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:44.510
and use that large
set of data to improve

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:46.494
the accuracy in our forecasts.

00:24:46.494 --> 00:24:47.452
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:24:47.452 --> 00:24:48.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:24:48.410 --> 00:24:51.680
Well, thank you so much for
taking the time to talk to us

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:54.710
and tell us about so many
cool predictions, really.

00:24:54.710 --> 00:24:56.240
That was really,
really interesting.

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:57.490
TIM KELTON: Well thanks, guys.

00:24:57.490 --> 00:24:58.160
I enjoyed it.

00:24:58.160 --> 00:24:58.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Thanks again, Tim,

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:00.440
for such an amazing interview.

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:03.730
I learned a lot about how you
can manage such a huge amount

00:25:03.730 --> 00:25:05.527
of data with preemptible VMs.

00:25:05.527 --> 00:25:07.360
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it
was super, super cool.

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:09.536
And just, like, the
large amount of storage

00:25:09.536 --> 00:25:11.660
and the amount of data that
they have moving around

00:25:11.660 --> 00:25:14.060
the system at any given point
in time is pretty incredible.

00:25:14.060 --> 00:25:15.893
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
it is pretty amazing.

00:25:15.893 --> 00:25:17.950
We'll have links.

00:25:17.950 --> 00:25:20.104
Actually, we'll have
images in the show notes,

00:25:20.104 --> 00:25:22.520
so you can have an idea of the
kind of maps they generates

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:24.370
if you're curious about it.

00:25:24.370 --> 00:25:28.070
But now it's time to go with
the question of the week.

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:31.112
And the question
of the week, I'm

00:25:31.112 --> 00:25:32.320
not sure where it comes from.

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:35.140
But I know that I had
it in the last GDG

00:25:35.140 --> 00:25:39.140
in San Francisco, which was
about Google Cloud Platform.

00:25:39.140 --> 00:25:41.460
One of our coworkers,
Terry, gave a talk.

00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:43.170
And at the end,
someone asked, OK,

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:45.630
so what are the things I can
do for free on Google Cloud

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:46.630
Platform?

00:25:46.630 --> 00:25:48.340
And the first part
of the question

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:51.530
is, well, when you sign up
for Google Cloud Platform,

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:55.165
you get already $300 that
are valid for two months.

00:25:55.165 --> 00:25:56.290
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, 60 days.

00:25:56.290 --> 00:25:58.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, 60 days.

00:25:58.110 --> 00:26:00.190
So with that, you
can do many things.

00:26:00.190 --> 00:26:02.870
Like, $300 is a decent
amount of money.

00:26:02.870 --> 00:26:04.860
You can run many instances
for quite a while.

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:07.235
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I'd generally
say people will probably

00:26:07.235 --> 00:26:09.900
run out of the 60 days before
they run out of the $300,

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:10.870
generally speaking.

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:11.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Generally speaking,

00:26:11.560 --> 00:26:13.450
yeah, unless you
go and you start,

00:26:13.450 --> 00:26:15.960
like, a 32 core instance
and stuff like that.

00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:17.590
That's likely to be more.

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:20.280
But otherwise, yeah, you
should be fine with that.

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:25.400
But even without signing
up and actually going

00:26:25.400 --> 00:26:29.060
through the free
trial, there's also

00:26:29.060 --> 00:26:33.010
some products that allow you to
have a free tier that is there

00:26:33.010 --> 00:26:34.550
forever, no limit of time.

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:37.440
So you can just be there
and use it for free.

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:39.790
And I actually do
it all the time.

00:26:39.790 --> 00:26:44.170
One of them is App Engine, which
is one of my favorite ones.

00:26:44.170 --> 00:26:49.170
So the free tier is 28 free
instance hours per day.

00:26:49.170 --> 00:26:51.800
MARK MANDEL: So you can
get like one point--

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:54.666
I'm not going to do the math--
slightly more than that.

00:26:54.666 --> 00:26:56.882
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I think
we all know how you--

00:26:56.882 --> 00:26:59.840
MARK MANDEL: I don't do
things in my head [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:03.300
Yeah, so slightly more than
a day for a frontend instance

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:04.450
is available to be used.

00:27:04.450 --> 00:27:05.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And the good
thing is that most of the time,

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:08.400
you're actually going to
use way less than that,

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:10.360
because if you don't
have that much traffic,

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:12.840
the instances will shut
down automatically.

00:27:12.840 --> 00:27:15.080
So in general, that
is way more than

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:18.530
enough for your personal
projects and stuff like that.

00:27:18.530 --> 00:27:19.790
So that is pretty awesome.

00:27:19.790 --> 00:27:21.870
And once you have
that, it is like, oh,

00:27:21.870 --> 00:27:24.650
so do I have access to
any other free things?

00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:26.280
I need to store data.

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:28.980
Well, you can use the
Datastore for free.

00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:33.570
And I think it is the
first gigabyte, right?

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:36.640
Yeah, the first gigabyte
of storage is free.

00:27:36.640 --> 00:27:41.550
And then you can do up to
50,000 reads, 20,000 writes,

00:27:41.550 --> 00:27:43.460
and 20,000 deletes.

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:45.120
And that is per day.

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:50.020
So 50,000 reads per day, it
is a decent amount of traffic.

00:27:50.020 --> 00:27:52.350
So you can check it out.

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:57.420
Also, you get some other things,
like mail and your fetch,

00:27:57.420 --> 00:27:58.090
and task queues.

00:27:58.090 --> 00:28:01.906
There is also different levels
of free tiers for those.

00:28:01.906 --> 00:28:03.280
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
and it's worth

00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:05.981
noting now with Datastore, it's
not just tied to App Engine

00:28:05.981 --> 00:28:06.480
anymore.

00:28:06.480 --> 00:28:08.650
So if you're using Datastore
outside of App Engine,

00:28:08.650 --> 00:28:10.540
you still sit into that
free tier, as well.

00:28:10.540 --> 00:28:12.956
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh yeah, you
can totally use the REST API

00:28:12.956 --> 00:28:16.490
and get that free tier, also.

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:17.320
What else?

00:28:17.320 --> 00:28:19.490
MARK MANDEL: So there
is also BigQuery,

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:21.870
one of our most favorite
big data processing

00:28:21.870 --> 00:28:25.180
tools, that gives you the
first terabyte of data

00:28:25.180 --> 00:28:26.770
process per month to be free.

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:27.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:28:27.300 --> 00:28:28.530
MARK MANDEL: Which is
really cool, especially

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:30.650
if you're playing with any
of our public data sets

00:28:30.650 --> 00:28:32.483
and you want to have a
go with some of those

00:28:32.483 --> 00:28:33.670
to see what's possible.

00:28:33.670 --> 00:28:35.277
You can do a fair
bit of processing

00:28:35.277 --> 00:28:37.110
without having to worry
about paying for it.

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:38.693
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
even if you don't

00:28:38.693 --> 00:28:40.450
want to use the
public data sets,

00:28:40.450 --> 00:28:42.350
you can also upload yours.

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:44.090
You don't pay for
uploading data.

00:28:44.090 --> 00:28:46.620
You pay for how much data
you're actually storing.

00:28:46.620 --> 00:28:48.990
But it's only $0.02
per gigabyte per month.

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:53.966
So you can get started quite
easily for not very much,

00:28:53.966 --> 00:28:55.590
especially taking
into account that you

00:28:55.590 --> 00:28:57.590
get those $300 for free.

00:28:57.590 --> 00:29:01.920
$300 divided by 0.02 is a lot.

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:02.420
Yeah.

00:29:02.420 --> 00:29:03.280
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, yeah, don't--

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:04.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Let's go with a lot.

00:29:04.160 --> 00:29:06.050
MARK MANDEL: Let's
go with a lot.

00:29:06.050 --> 00:29:07.556
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Then what else?

00:29:07.556 --> 00:29:08.555
MARK MANDEL: Vision API.

00:29:08.555 --> 00:29:10.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh
yeah, the Vision API.

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:13.250
Vision API, it is really cool.

00:29:13.250 --> 00:29:15.000
It's one of my favorite
APIs, because it

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:17.350
allows you to do things that
basically seem like magic.

00:29:17.350 --> 00:29:18.600
MARK MANDEL: It's magic, yeah.

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:20.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
it's basically magic.

00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:23.720
And everything you need to
know is how to use JSON.

00:29:23.720 --> 00:29:24.764
So it's pretty amazing.

00:29:24.764 --> 00:29:26.430
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
and it's really cool.

00:29:26.430 --> 00:29:29.030
So it doesn't necessarily
work per image.

00:29:29.030 --> 00:29:30.180
It works per feature.

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:32.920
So you're asking an image,
say, can we label detection

00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:34.320
or OCR this text.

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:36.480
So you could do multiple
features per image.

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:39.380
But the first 1,000 units per
month is free per feature.

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:42.154
So you can get a decent
amount of stuff done there.

00:29:42.154 --> 00:29:43.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, I could say

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:45.164
that it is not a huge amount.

00:29:45.164 --> 00:29:47.330
So if you have an application
that is actually used,

00:29:47.330 --> 00:29:49.850
you will hit that
limit quite quick.

00:29:49.850 --> 00:29:51.397
But this is perfect
for development.

00:29:51.397 --> 00:29:53.980
If you're doing development and
testing, this is totally fine,

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:57.040
and you will probably
not hit it anytime soon.

00:29:57.040 --> 00:29:59.430
So that is really cool.

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:01.280
Then we have a bunch
of other things

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:03.527
that they're not really free.

00:30:03.527 --> 00:30:05.110
MARK MANDEL: But
they're cheap enough.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:08.580
Like, they round down
to zero, I feel like.

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:10.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: One of
the examples is Pub/Sub.

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:14.970
Pub/Sub is 200
million operations.

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:17.292
It is $0.40.

00:30:17.292 --> 00:30:24.159
So basically, one billion
operations is about one coffee.

00:30:24.159 --> 00:30:25.200
MARK MANDEL: I like that.

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:28.157
We should do more
stuff per coffee.

00:30:28.157 --> 00:30:29.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
actually really depends.

00:30:29.990 --> 00:30:32.320
If you're drinking
coffee in San Francisco,

00:30:32.320 --> 00:30:35.600
